<article class="env-definition" id="00D3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00D3">Definition <span data-tag="00D3">4.21.1</span>.</a> Let $I$ be a set and let $\leq $ be a binary relation on $I$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say $\leq $ is a <em>preorder</em> if it is transitive (if $i \leq j$ and $j \leq k$ then $i \leq k$) and reflexive ($i \leq i$ for all $i \in I$). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>preordered set</em> is a set endowed with a preorder. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>directed set</em> is a preordered set $(I, \leq )$ such that $I$ is not empty and such that $\forall i, j \in I$, there exists $k \in I$ with $i \leq k, j \leq k$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $\leq $ is a <em>partial order</em> if it is a preorder which is antisymmetric (if $i \leq j$ and $j \leq i$, then $i = j$). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>partially ordered set</em> is a set endowed with a partial order. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>directed partially ordered set</em> is a directed set whose ordering is a partial order. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>